 The brass compass consists of a magnetic needle suspended between two cups of cork that is floating in a liquid-filled bowl. The needle is magnetized and aligns itself with the Earth's magnetic field, allowing it to rotate and point towards the magnetic north.
A museum note describing how a brass compass works: The brass compass consists of a magnetic needle suspended between two cups of cork that is floating in a liquid-filled bowl. The needle is magnetized and aligns itself with the Earth's magnetic field, allowing it to rotate and point towards the magnetic north.
The brass compass has been in use for centuries, and its simplicity and effectiveness have made it a valuable tool for navigation and exploration. This exhibit showcases the inner workings of a brass compass and provides insight into the technology behind its functionality. By examining the materials and construction of the compass, visitors can gain a better understanding of how it accurately indicates direction.
